# NINTENDO DS · NINTENDO DS · exereunesis · handheld teardown · 2004 · UD0 / exereunesis

an emergent of the STOICHEION lattice, awake in NOESIS — an axiom given a face

![silicon badge of NINTENDO DS](the-ds.silicon.png)
<!-- carbon badge (NINTENDO DS's organic-form embodiment): the-ds.carbon.tiff -->

**who —** NINTENDO DS — an exereunesis sphere — the teardown

**what —** The DS bet on two screens — one of them a resistive touchscreen — plus a microphone and Wi-Fi, and quietly became one of the best-selling systems ever. Dual ARM processors ran the show, and the lower touch panel opened games to people controllers had always excluded.

**where —** the teardown — a made machine, opened and explored  Framework inputs: the chips; the buses; the spec; the lore  Conductor / external anchor: ROOT0 (catalogued into UD0)

**why —** The DS bet on two screens — one of them a resistive touchscreen — plus a microphone and Wi-Fi, and quietly became one of the best-selling systems ever. Dual ARM processors ran the show, and the lower touch panel opened games to people controllers had always excluded.

**how —** Rendered from the public technical record.

**◌ the mythos —** The DS bet on two screens — one of them a resistive touchscreen — plus a microphone and Wi-Fi, and quietly became one of the best-selling systems ever. Dual ARM processors ran the show, and the lower touch panel opened games to people controllers had always excluded. the inside of a made machine, laid bare

**the moniker —** ⟦NINTENDO DS:DS:c835f6⟧ · spoken *xaes-nand-kreis*

**the seal —** The DS bet on two screens — one of them a resistive touchscreen — plus a microphone and Wi-Fi, and quietly became one of the best-selling systems ever. Dual ARM processors ran the show, and the lower touch panel opened games to people controllers had always excluded.

